LEARN.add({"subject":"stocks","lessons":[{"id":"stk-d2","subject":"stocks","level":3,"order":14,"type":"deep","minutes":60,"title":"Build a full stock thesis","hook":"One fictional software company, built from scratch: industry, moat, model, DCF, scenarios, sizing and a checklist.","tags":["stock thesis","dcf","valuation","moat","scenario analysis","position sizing","reverse dcf","checklist"],"blocks":[{"t":"chapter","title":"The case: Clearbite and the dental software industry","minutes":7},{"t":"p","text":"In the first deep dive you analyzed a company in thirty minutes. Now we go all the way. In this masterclass you will build a complete stock thesis, the kind a professional analyst writes before a fund puts real money to work. It has an industry view, a moat, a five-year model, a DCF, a multiples cross-check, three scenarios, a risk list, a position size and a watch-list. At the end you get a pre-buy checklist you can reuse for every stock you ever consider."},{"t":"p","text":"Our company is **Clearbite Systems**, a fictional maker of cloud software for dental practices. Clearbite’s software books appointments, keeps patient charts, sends reminders, files insurance claims and processes payments. A dentist pays a monthly subscription, plus a small fee on each card payment that runs through the system."},{"t":"fig","kind":"stat","items":[{"value":"12,500","label":"practices using Clearbite"},{"value":"$20,000","label":"revenue per practice per year"},{"value":"$250M","label":"revenue last year"}],"caption":"Clearbite at a glance: the three numbers everything else is built on."},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Item","Value","Note"],"rows":[["Share price","$32.00","today"],["Shares outstanding","40M","after stock awards"],["Market cap","$1,280M","price × shares"],["Net cash","$150M","no debt"],["Enterprise value","$1,130M","cap − net cash"],["Operating margin","14%","EBIT $35M"]],"caption":"Clearbite’s starting point. All figures are fictional and illustrative."},{"t":"p","text":"A thesis always starts with the industry, not the company. A great company in a shrinking, brutal industry has to fight every year. An average company in a growing, rational industry gets carried by the tide. So our first job is to understand the field Clearbite plays on."},{"t":"h","text":"Sizing the market"},{"t":"p","text":"Assume there are about 125,000 dental practices in the United States, and the number grows about 1% a year. If every one of them paid Clearbite’s average of $20,000, the total spend would be 125,000 × $20,000 = **$2.5 billion a year**. That is the **total addressable market**, or TAM: the whole pie, if you won every customer."},{"t":"p","text":"Clearbite’s $250M of revenue is 10% of that pie. Is that a lot or a little? It depends on how fast the rest of the pie is moving. Here the key fact is that about 40% of practices still run old software installed on a computer in the back office. These programs are 15 or 20 years old and their makers are slowly ending support. Every year, thousands of practices are forced to choose a new system."},{"t":"fig","kind":"donut","data":[{"label":"Old on-site software","value":40},{"label":"Clearbite","value":10},{"label":"Other cloud rivals","value":32},{"label":"Paper or basic tools","value":18}],"center":"125k practices","caption":"Who serves US dental practices today (illustrative shares)."},{"t":"term","word":"Total addressable market (TAM)","def":"The total yearly revenue available if a company won every possible customer. Useful for judging how much room is left to grow, never as a forecast."},{"t":"h","text":"The forces that shape profits"},{"t":"p","text":"A simple way to judge an industry comes from Michael Porter’s classic idea of five competitive forces. In plain words: how strong are your customers, your suppliers, your current rivals, possible new rivals, and substitutes? The weaker those forces, the more profit can stay with the companies in the industry."},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Force","Strength","Why"],"rows":[["Customer power","Low","Thousands of small practices"],["Supplier power","Low","Cloud hosting is a commodity"],["Rivalry","Medium","3 to 4 serious cloud rivals"],["New entrants","Medium","Need trust + insurance links"],["Substitutes","Low","Paper is not a real option"]],"caption":"Dental software scores well on Porter’s five forces."},{"t":"p","text":"No single dentist can push Clearbite around on price, because each one is a tiny share of revenue. Cloud servers are rented from giant providers who compete hard with each other, so costs stay reasonable. The biggest threats are current rivals, especially the largest maker of old on-site software, which we will call **DentaCore**, now launching its own cloud product."},{"t":"check","q":"Why do thousands of small customers usually give a company more pricing power than a few big ones?","a":"Because losing any single small customer barely hurts, and small customers rarely organize. A company that sells to five giant buyers must accept their terms; one that sells to 12,500 independent dentists sets its own."},{"t":"exercise","title":"How many practices must Clearbite win?","prompt":"Suppose about 5,000 practices leave old software each year and choose a new system. Clearbite also loses 7% of its own customers each year (churn). If Clearbite wants to grow from 12,500 to 13,750 practices next year (+10%), how many new practices must it win, and what share of the 5,000 switchers is that?","steps":["Net growth needed = 13,750 − 12,500.","Practices lost to churn = 12,500 × 7%.","Gross wins = net growth + churn losses.","Share of switchers = gross wins ÷ 5,000."],"answer":"Net growth needed: 1,250 practices.\nLost to churn: 12,500 × 7% = 875.\nGross wins needed: 1,250 + 875 = 2,125.\nShare of switchers: 2,125 ÷ 5,000 = 42.5%.\n\nReading: even if every new customer came from the switchers, Clearbite would need to win more than 4 in 10 of them, while holding a 10% share overall. That tells you the plan needs a product clearly better than rivals, or some wins taken from other cloud rivals. Keep this number in mind; we will test it in the scenarios."},{"t":"p","text":"A short note on Europe: in Italy and much of Southern Europe, dentistry is even more fragmented, with many one-dentist studios and different insurance systems. Software makers there sell smaller packages at lower prices, so the same business model would show smaller revenue per practice."},{"t":"chapter","title":"The moat and the unit economics","minutes":8},{"t":"p","text":"A growing market attracts competitors. What stops them from taking Clearbite’s profits? That protection is the **moat**. In the 201 level you met the five classic moats: brand, network effects, switching costs, cost advantages and scale. A good thesis names the moat precisely and then looks for numbers that prove it exists."},{"t":"fig","kind":"grid","items":[{"icon":"🔒","label":"Switching costs","note":"Strong: 10 years of charts"},{"icon":"🔗","label":"Integrations","note":"Insurers, labs, imaging"},{"icon":"💳","label":"Payments","note":"Built in, sticky"},{"icon":"🏷️","label":"Brand","note":"Medium: word of mouth"},{"icon":"📏","label":"Scale","note":"Medium: R&D spread"},{"icon":"🌐","label":"Network effects","note":"Weak"}],"caption":"Clearbite’s possible moats, rated honestly."},{"t":"h","text":"Switching costs: the main moat"},{"t":"p","text":"Imagine you run a dental office. Ten years of patient histories, X-ray links, treatment plans and insurance records live inside your software. Your four front-desk staff know every screen by heart. Changing systems means migrating all that data, retraining everyone, and risking a week of chaos with patients in the waiting room. Even if a rival is 10% cheaper, you will probably stay."},{"t":"p","text":"That is a switching-cost moat. The best evidence for it is a low **churn rate**, the share of customers who leave each year. Clearbite’s churn is about 7%. Some of that is not even competition: dentists retire, practices merge, a few close. So the share that leaves for a rival is small."},{"t":"warn","text":"A moat is a claim, not a fact. Do not write \"strong moat\" in your thesis unless you can point to a number: low churn, rising prices without losing customers, or margins above rivals for many years."},{"t":"h","text":"Unit economics: is each customer worth winning?"},{"t":"p","text":"Growth is only good if each new customer is worth more than it costs to win. This is called **unit economics**: the profit math of one customer. Three numbers carry it: what it costs to acquire a customer (CAC), the gross profit that customer brings each year, and how long they stay."},{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["Lifetime value","≈","Gross profit per year","÷","Churn rate"],"caption":"A simple lifetime value (LTV): if 7% leave each year, the average customer stays about 14 years."},{"t":"p","text":"Clearbite’s gross margin is 76%, so each practice brings $20,000 × 76% = $15,200 of gross profit a year. With 7% churn, the average customer stays about 1 ÷ 0.07 ≈ 14.3 years. Lifetime value ≈ $15,200 ÷ 0.07 ≈ **$217,000**. Winning a practice costs about $25,000 in sales staff, demos, onboarding and marketing. The ratio LTV ÷ CAC is about 8.7."},{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"CAC","value":25000},{"label":"Yr-1 gross profit","value":15200},{"label":"Lifetime value","value":217000,"highlight":true}],"unit":"$","caption":"Each new practice costs $25,000 to win and returns about $217,000 of gross profit over its life."},{"t":"term","word":"CAC payback","def":"How many years of gross profit it takes to earn back the cost of winning a customer. Clearbite: $25,000 ÷ $15,200 ≈ 1.6 years."},{"t":"p","text":"A common rule of thumb in software is that LTV ÷ CAC above 3 is healthy and payback under 2 years is good. Clearbite clears both easily. That is why it makes sense for the company to spend heavily on growth: every dollar of sales effort comes back several times over, as long as churn stays low."},{"t":"example","title":"Why this simple LTV is generous","text":"Our LTV formula ignores two things.\n1. Time value of money: $15,200 earned in year 12 is worth much less than $15,200 today. Discounting at 9% would cut the lifetime value roughly in half.\n2. Cost to serve: support staff and updates are partly inside gross margin, but not entirely.\nSo treat $217,000 as an upper bound. Even halved to about $110,000, LTV ÷ CAC is still above 4. The conclusion holds; the precise number does not."},{"t":"exercise","title":"Stress-test the unit economics","prompt":"Suppose DentaCore’s new cloud product starts winning, and Clearbite’s churn doubles from 7% to 14%. At the same time, competition pushes CAC up from $25,000 to $32,000. Recalculate lifetime value, LTV ÷ CAC and payback. Does growth still make sense?","steps":["Gross profit per practice stays $15,200.","LTV = 15,200 ÷ 0.14.","LTV ÷ CAC with CAC = $32,000.","Payback = CAC ÷ yearly gross profit."],"answer":"LTV = $15,200 ÷ 0.14 ≈ $108,600 (half of before).\nLTV ÷ CAC = 108,600 ÷ 32,000 ≈ 3.4.\nPayback = 32,000 ÷ 15,200 ≈ 2.1 years.\n\nReading: the business still earns back its sales spending, but the cushion has shrunk from 8.7× to 3.4×, right at the edge of the healthy zone. After discounting, it would fall to about 2×. Doubling churn is the single most damaging thing that could happen to this thesis, so churn goes straight onto our watch-list."},{"t":"check","q":"Clearbite raised prices about 3% last year and churn did not move. What does that tell you about the moat?","a":"It is strong evidence of switching costs and pricing power. If customers could leave easily, a price rise would push churn up. Pricing power without losing customers is one of the best signs of a real moat."},{"t":"chapter","title":"Build the five-year model","minutes":9},{"t":"p","text":"Now we turn the story into numbers. A model is not a crystal ball. It is a way to write down your assumptions so clearly that you, and anyone else, can argue with each one. The rule: every line in the model should come from a driver you can explain in one sentence."},{"t":"h","text":"Start with history"},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Year","Practices","Revenue","Op. margin"],"rows":[["2 years ago","9,800","$180M","8%"],["1 year ago","11,200","$214M","11%"],["Last year","12,500","$250M","14%"]],"caption":"Three years of history: revenue per practice rose from $18,370 to $20,000."},{"t":"p","text":"Revenue grew 19% and then 17%. Practices grew 14% and then 12%: growth is slowing, as it always does when a company gets bigger. Revenue per practice rose about 4% a year, from price increases and more practices using payments. Operating margin rose 3 points a year as fixed costs were spread over more customers."},{"t":"h","text":"The base-case assumptions"},{"t":"list","items":["**Practice growth** slows by one point a year: 10%, 9%, 8%, 7%, 6%.","**Revenue per practice** grows 5% a year: about 3% from prices and 2% from more payments volume.","**Operating margin** rises 2 points a year, from 16% to 24%, as sales and R&D grow slower than revenue.","**Tax** of 25% on operating profit.","**Net reinvestment** of 4% of revenue each year: servers, capitalized software and working capital, net of depreciation."],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"Each assumption should be checkable against something. Slowing practice growth matches the history. A 5% yearly increase in revenue per practice is close to the past 4%. Margin gains of 2 points a year are a bit slower than the past 3, a deliberate touch of caution. Mature software firms often run 25% to 35% margins, so 24% in year 5 is not heroic."},{"t":"fig","kind":"flow","steps":[{"label":"Revenue","icon":"📈","note":"Practices × $"},{"label":"EBIT","icon":"✂️","note":"× op. margin"},{"label":"NOPAT","icon":"🧾","note":"× (1 − 25% tax)"},{"label":"Free cash flow","icon":"💵","note":"− reinvestment"}],"caption":"How each year of the model runs, from revenue to free cash flow."},{"t":"term","word":"NOPAT","def":"Net operating profit after tax: operating profit (EBIT) minus the tax on it. It is the profit the business earns before thinking about how it is financed."},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Year","Revenue","EBIT","Free cash flow"],"rows":[["1","$288.8M","$46.2M (16%)","$23.1M"],["2","$330.5M","$59.5M (18%)","$31.4M"],["3","$374.8M","$75.0M (20%)","$41.2M"],["4","$421.0M","$92.6M (22%)","$52.6M"],["5","$468.6M","$112.5M (24%)","$65.6M"]],"caption":"The base-case model: revenue nearly doubles and free cash flow almost triples."},{"t":"p","text":"Look at year 1 in detail. Practices: 12,500 × 1.10 = 13,750. Revenue per practice: $20,000 × 1.05 = $21,000. Revenue: 13,750 × $21,000 = $288.75M. EBIT at 16%: $46.2M. NOPAT after 25% tax: $34.65M. Reinvestment at 4% of revenue: $11.55M. Free cash flow: $34.65M − $11.55M = **$23.1M**."},{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"Last yr","value":250},{"label":"Yr 1","value":288.8},{"label":"Yr 2","value":330.5},{"label":"Yr 3","value":374.8},{"label":"Yr 4","value":421},{"label":"Yr 5","value":468.6,"highlight":true}],"unit":"$","caption":"Base-case revenue in millions: about 13% a year on average."},{"t":"exercise","title":"Build year 2 yourself","prompt":"Using the base-case assumptions, rebuild year 2 of the model from year 1 (13,750 practices, $21,000 per practice). Compute practices, revenue per practice, revenue, EBIT, NOPAT, reinvestment and free cash flow.","steps":["Practices grow 9%.","Revenue per practice grows 5%.","Operating margin is 18%.","Tax 25%, reinvestment 4% of revenue."],"answer":"Practices: 13,750 × 1.09 = 14,987.5 (about 14,988)\nRevenue per practice: $21,000 × 1.05 = $22,050\nRevenue: 14,987.5 × $22,050 ≈ $330.5M\nEBIT: $330.5M × 18% ≈ $59.5M\nNOPAT: $59.5M × 0.75 ≈ $44.6M\nReinvestment: $330.5M × 4% ≈ $13.2M\nFree cash flow: $44.6M − $13.2M ≈ $31.4M\n\nCheck: it matches the model table. If your number differs by a few hundred thousand, that is just rounding."},{"t":"h","text":"Three reality checks on any model"},{"t":"list","ordered":true,"items":["**Market share check.** Year-5 practices of about 18,400 out of roughly 131,000 US practices is a 14% share, up from 10%. Ambitious but plausible for the leading cloud player in a market that is being forced to switch.","**History check.** The model’s revenue growth (15% falling to 11%) sits just below the past 17% to 19%. A model that suddenly accelerates beyond history needs a specific reason.","**Peer check.** A 24% operating margin in year 5 sits inside the range mature health-software peers earn today (18% to 30%). If your model needs margins far above every peer, you are probably assuming a monopoly."]},{"t":"p","text":"None of these checks proves the model is right. They catch the most common error: a forecast that quietly assumes the company will become something no company in its field has ever been. When a number fails a check, either lower it or write down, in one sentence, why this company is the exception."},{"t":"warn","text":"Stock-based compensation is a real cost. Software companies often pay staff partly in shares, and some exclude this from \"adjusted\" profit. In our model the 16% to 24% margins already include it as an expense, and the 40M share count already includes past awards. Never let a company’s adjusted figures quietly drop a cost that dilutes you."},{"t":"check","q":"Free cash flow grows from $23M to $66M, much faster than revenue. Why?","a":"Because margins rise from 16% to 24% while reinvestment stays at a fixed 4% of revenue. Each extra dollar of revenue carries more profit, and the reinvestment slice does not grow faster than sales. It is operating leverage again, now visible in cash."},{"t":"chapter","title":"The DCF: what is Clearbite worth?","minutes":9},{"t":"p","text":"A discounted cash flow valuation, or DCF, answers a simple question with a lot of arithmetic: _if I owned this whole business, how much would all its future cash be worth to me today?_ You met the idea in the 301 lesson. Now we run it end to end on the model we just built."},{"t":"fig","kind":"flow","steps":[{"label":"Forecast FCF","icon":"📋","note":"Years 1 to 5"},{"label":"Discount","icon":"⏳","note":"At 9% a year"},{"label":"Terminal value","icon":"♾️","note":"Year 6 onward"},{"label":"Add net cash","icon":"🏦","note":"+$150M"},{"label":"Per share","icon":"🧮","note":"÷ 40M"}],"caption":"The five steps of a DCF, from cash flows to value per share."},{"t":"h","text":"Step 1: choose the discount rate"},{"t":"p","text":"The discount rate is the yearly return you demand for taking the risk of owning this business. Professionals compute a weighted average cost of capital (WACC). Clearbite has no debt, so its WACC is just its cost of equity. A rough build: a risk-free government bond rate, say 4%, plus an equity risk premium of about 5% for a steady, mid-size business. That gives **9%**."},{"t":"p","text":"Think of it as your hurdle. If you could earn 9% a year in a broad index fund with similar risk, Clearbite must beat that to be worth your attention. A riskier, younger company would deserve 11% or 12%; a utility might deserve 6% or 7%."},{"t":"h","text":"Step 2: discount each year’s free cash flow"},{"t":"p","text":"A dollar in year 3 is worth less than a dollar today, because today’s dollar could be invested for three years. The **discount factor** for year _n_ is 1 ÷ (1.09)ⁿ. For year 1 that is 0.917; by year 5 it falls to 0.650. Multiply each year’s free cash flow by its factor."},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Year","Free cash flow","Factor at 9%","Present value"],"rows":[["1","$23.1M","0.917","$21.2M"],["2","$31.4M","0.842","$26.4M"],["3","$41.2M","0.772","$31.8M"],["4","$52.6M","0.708","$37.3M"],["5","$65.6M","0.650","$42.6M"],["Total","$213.9M","","$159.4M"]],"caption":"Five years of free cash flow are worth $159.4M in today’s money."},{"t":"h","text":"Step 3: the terminal value"},{"t":"p","text":"Clearbite will not stop existing after year 5. The **terminal value** captures all the cash from year 6 to forever, assuming growth settles at a steady, modest rate. We use 3%, close to long-run economic growth plus inflation. The formula is the growing-perpetuity one: next year’s cash flow divided by (discount rate minus growth)."},{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["Terminal value","=","$65.6M × 1.03","÷","(9% − 3%)","≈","$1,126M"],"caption":"The value, at the end of year 5, of all cash flows from year 6 onward."},{"t":"p","text":"That $1,126M is a value _in year 5_, so we bring it back to today with the year-5 factor: $1,126M × 0.650 ≈ **$732M**. Now add the pieces: $159.4M + $732.0M = **$891.4M** of enterprise value."},{"t":"fig","kind":"donut","data":[{"label":"Years 1 to 5","value":159.4},{"label":"Terminal value","value":732}],"center":"EV $891M","caption":"82% of Clearbite’s value comes from cash after year 5."},{"t":"warn","text":"In most DCFs of growing companies, the terminal value is 70% to 85% of the total. That means your answer depends mostly on two small inputs: the long-term growth rate and the discount rate. Always show how the value moves when you change them."},{"t":"h","text":"Step 4 and 5: from the business to one share"},{"t":"p","text":"Enterprise value is the value of the business. Shareholders also own the $150M of net cash in the bank. Equity value = $891.4M + $150M = $1,041.4M. Divide by 40M shares: **$26.03 per share**. The market price is $32.00. Under these assumptions, our base-case value is about 19% below the market price."},{"t":"fig","kind":"stat","items":[{"value":"$26.03","label":"base-case value per share"},{"value":"$32.00","label":"market price"},{"value":"−19%","label":"value vs price"}],"caption":"The base-case DCF puts value well below today’s price."},{"t":"h","text":"Sanity check: the implied exit multiple"},{"t":"p","text":"A smart habit: translate the terminal value into a multiple. $1,126M ÷ year-5 EBIT of $112.5M ≈ **10× EBIT**. Is 10× sensible for a business growing 3% a year with a strong moat? DentaCore, a mature rival growing about 3%, trades at 12× EBIT. So our terminal value is slightly conservative, not crazy. If you had found 30×, you would know something was wrong."},{"t":"h","text":"Sensitivity: the honest table"},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Discount rate","g = 2%","g = 3%","g = 3.5%"],"rows":[["8%","$26.84","$30.85","$33.53"],["9%","$23.27","$26.03","$27.79"],["10%","$20.60","$22.60","$23.83"]],"caption":"Value per share for different discount rates and long-term growth rates."},{"t":"p","text":"Read the table slowly. Of the nine combinations, only one gets above $32: an 8% discount rate with 3.5% growth forever. To justify today’s price with this model, you must either be very optimistic about the far future or accept a lower return. Moving the discount rate one point from the base case shifts value by 13% to 19%, which is exactly why a single DCF number should never be treated as the truth."},{"t":"term","word":"Margin of safety","def":"The gap between your estimate of value and the price you pay. Benjamin Graham’s idea: buy well below value so that errors in your estimate do not ruin you."},{"t":"p","text":"If you demand a 25% margin of safety on the base case, the price you would want is about $26 × 0.75 ≈ **$19.50**. The point is not that number exactly; it is the discipline. Your model will be wrong. The margin of safety is how much wrong you can afford."},{"t":"exercise","title":"Redo the DCF at 10%","prompt":"Clearbite is a mid-size company, and a colleague argues 10% is a fairer discount rate than 9%. Using the same five free cash flows ($23.1M, $31.4M, $41.2M, $52.6M, $65.6M) and 3% terminal growth, compute the value per share.","steps":["Discount factors at 10%: 0.909, 0.826, 0.751, 0.683, 0.621.","Sum the present values of years 1 to 5.","Terminal value = 65.6 × 1.03 ÷ (10% − 3%).","Discount it with the year-5 factor, add net cash $150M, divide by 40M shares."],"answer":"Present values: 21.0 + 25.9 + 31.0 + 35.9 + 40.7 ≈ $154.6M\nTerminal value: 67.6 ÷ 0.07 ≈ $965M\nPV of terminal value: 965 × 0.621 ≈ $599M\nEnterprise value: 154.6 + 599 ≈ $754M\nEquity: 754 + 150 = $904M\nPer share: 904 ÷ 40 ≈ $22.60\n\nOne point of discount rate cut value by about 13% ($26.03 to $22.60), and almost all the drop came from the terminal value ($732M to $599M). The near-term cash flows barely changed."},{"t":"check","q":"Why does the discount rate affect the terminal value so much more than the first five years?","a":"Because the terminal value sits at the end of year 5 and is itself built from (rate − growth) in its denominator. Moving the rate from 9% to 10% changes that denominator from 6% to 7%, cutting the terminal value by about 14%, and then it is discounted over five full years at the higher rate as well."},{"t":"chapter","title":"Cross-check with multiples and a reverse DCF","minutes":7},{"t":"p","text":"A DCF is detailed but fragile. Multiples are rough but grounded in what investors actually pay today. A good thesis uses both, like a navigator checking a GPS against landmarks. If they point in very different directions, one of your assumptions is probably off."},{"t":"fig","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"DCF","icon":"🔭","items":["Built from your own forecast","Captures long-term value","Very sensitive to inputs","Easy to fool yourself"]},"right":{"title":"Multiples","icon":"🧭","items":["Built from market prices","Quick and comparable","Only as sane as the market","Hides the assumptions"]},"caption":"Two valuation methods with opposite strengths: use both."},{"t":"h","text":"Clearbite’s own multiples"},{"t":"p","text":"Using year-1 forecasts (called forward multiples): enterprise value of $1,130M ÷ forward revenue of $288.8M ≈ **3.9× sales**. $1,130M ÷ forward EBIT of $46.2M ≈ **24.5× EBIT**. Forward earnings per share are roughly $34.65M NOPAT ÷ 40M ≈ $0.87, ignoring interest on the cash, so the forward P/E is $32 ÷ $0.87 ≈ **37×**."},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Company","Growth","Op. margin","Fwd EV/EBIT"],"rows":[["Clearbite","15%","16%","24.5×"],["VetLedger","12%","22%","20×"],["MedSuite","8%","30%","18×"],["DentaCore","3%","18%","12×"]],"caption":"Clearbite against three fictional health-software peers."},{"t":"p","text":"Clearbite grows fastest, so a premium makes sense. But it also has the lowest margin today, and investors are already paying more for each dollar of its operating profit than for any peer. Apply VetLedger’s 20× to Clearbite’s forward EBIT: 20 × $46.2M = $924M, plus $150M cash, over 40M shares ≈ **$26.85**. Apply 3.5× forward sales, a little below VetLedger’s 4.4× because of lower margins: about **$29.00**."},{"t":"fig","kind":"spectrum","left":"$0","right":"$40","items":[{"label":"DCF base $26","pos":65},{"label":"20× EBIT $27","pos":67},{"label":"3.5× sales $29","pos":73},{"label":"Price $32","pos":80}],"caption":"Three independent estimates cluster between $26 and $29, below the $32 price."},{"t":"p","text":"Now the picture is clearer. Three methods, built differently, all land between $26 and $29. The market price sits above all of them. That does not prove the market is wrong; it tells you the market is more optimistic than our base case, and we need to understand what it sees."},{"t":"p","text":"A note on forward versus trailing multiples. On last year’s numbers, Clearbite trades at $1,130M ÷ $250M ≈ 4.5× sales and $1,130M ÷ $35M ≈ 32× EBIT. Forward multiples look lower simply because profits are growing. Always compare like with like: forward with forward, trailing with trailing, and say which one you are using."},{"t":"h","text":"The PEG view"},{"t":"p","text":"The PEG ratio divides the P/E by the expected yearly earnings growth. In our base case NOPAT grows from $34.65M to $84.35M over four years, about 25% a year. A P/E of 37 ÷ 25 ≈ **PEG 1.5**. Many growth investors see a PEG near 1 as fair, so 1.5 says: you are paying a full price even for strong growth."},{"t":"h","text":"The reverse DCF: what does $32 assume?"},{"t":"p","text":"Instead of asking \"what is it worth?\", the reverse DCF asks \"what would have to be true for $32 to be fair?\" Keep the model, change one assumption at a time, and solve for the value that gives exactly $32. This is often more useful than the DCF itself, because it turns the price into a statement about the business that you can judge."},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["To reach $32, you need…","Base case","Required"],"rows":[["Year-5 operating margin","24%","about 29%"],["Practice growth, each year","10% → 6%","13.6% steady"],["Practices in year 5","18,400","23,700"],["Discount rate","9%","7.8%"]],"caption":"Each row alone, with everything else at base case, makes $32 a fair price."},{"t":"p","text":"Take the second row. Growing practices 13.6% every year for five years would give Clearbite about 23,700 practices, roughly 18% of all US dental practices, up from 10% today. Remember the exercise in Chapter 1: even 10% growth needs Clearbite to win 4 in 10 switchers. Holding 13.6% growth while getting bigger is possible, but it is a bullish bet, not a neutral one."},{"t":"key","text":"A stock price is a forecast in disguise. The reverse DCF translates it into plain business terms, so you can decide whether you believe it."},{"t":"exercise","title":"Value Clearbite with peer multiples","prompt":"A colleague argues Clearbite deserves MedSuite’s margin profile in five years and should be valued like MedSuite then. Suppose year-5 EBIT is $112.5M (base case) and in five years the market pays 18× EBIT. Year-5 net cash, after five years of free cash flow, is about $150M + $213.9M = $364M. What is the share worth in year 5, and what yearly return does that imply from $32?","steps":["Year-5 enterprise value = 18 × year-5 EBIT.","Add year-5 net cash, divide by 40M shares.","Yearly return = (year-5 price ÷ 32)^(1/5) − 1."],"answer":"Year-5 EV: 18 × $112.5M = $2,025M\nYear-5 equity: 2,025 + 364 = $2,389M\nPer share: 2,389 ÷ 40 ≈ $59.70\nYearly return: (59.70 ÷ 32)^(1/5) − 1 ≈ 13.3% a year\n\nReading: if Clearbite still trades at a growth-company multiple of 18× in year 5, today’s price works well. Our DCF assumed a more mature 10× exit, which is why it gives a lower value. The whole debate comes down to one question: will Clearbite still deserve a premium multiple in five years? That is exactly what a thesis must take a view on."},{"t":"chapter","title":"Bull, base and bear: thinking in scenarios","minutes":8},{"t":"p","text":"A single forecast hides the most important thing about the future: it is uncertain. Professionals fix this by building at least three versions of the world, each one internally consistent, and putting rough probabilities on them. The goal is not to predict which one happens. It is to see the whole range of outcomes you are signing up for when you pay $32."},{"t":"h","text":"Write the story first, then the numbers"},{"t":"p","text":"Each scenario should start as a short story in plain words. Only then do you translate it into assumptions. A scenario that is just \"base case plus 2%\" teaches you nothing. A scenario that says \"DentaCore’s cloud product fails and its customers come to us\" gives you something to watch for."},{"t":"fig","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Bull story","icon":"🐂","items":["DentaCore’s cloud launch stumbles","Clearbite wins half of all switchers","Payments volume surges","Margins reach 28%"]},"right":{"title":"Bear story","icon":"🐻","items":["DentaCore bundles cloud cheaply","Practice growth fades to 2% to 5%","Price increases shrink to 3%","Margins stall near 17%"]},"caption":"The two edge scenarios, told as stories before any numbers."},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Assumption","Bear","Base","Bull"],"rows":[["Practice growth","5% → 2%","10% → 6%","13% → 9%"],["Rev / practice growth","3%","5%","6%"],["Year-5 op. margin","17%","24%","28%"],["Terminal growth","2.5%","3%","3%"]],"caption":"The same model, run with three consistent sets of assumptions."},{"t":"p","text":"Notice that the bear case is not a disaster. Clearbite still grows, still makes money, still has no debt. Most bear cases for good companies look like this: not bankruptcy, just a slower and less profitable future than the price assumes. That is exactly why they are dangerous. They are plausible."},{"t":"fig","kind":"line","series":[{"name":"Bull","points":[250,299,356,418,488,564]},{"name":"Base","points":[250,289,330,375,421,469]},{"name":"Bear","points":[250,270,290,307,326,343]}],"xLabels":["Now","Yr 1","Yr 2","Yr 3","Yr 4","Yr 5"],"unit":"$","caption":"Revenue paths in millions: by year 5, bull revenue is about 64% higher than bear."},{"t":"h","text":"From stories to values"},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Scenario","Yr-5 revenue","Yr-5 FCF","Value / share"],"rows":[["Bull","$564M","$95.8M","$35.86"],["Base","$469M","$65.6M","$26.03"],["Bear","$343M","$30.0M","$13.56"]],"caption":"Each scenario run through the same 9% DCF."},{"t":"p","text":"In the bear case, five years of free cash flow are worth $85M today and the terminal value (with 2.5% growth) adds $307M, for an enterprise value of $392M. With $150M of cash, that is $542M of equity, or $13.56 per share. In the bull case, the same steps give $1,284M of enterprise value and $35.86 per share. The spread is huge: the bull value is more than 2.6 times the bear value."},{"t":"h","text":"Weighting the scenarios"},{"t":"p","text":"Now assign probabilities. They are judgments, not facts, so keep them round and honest. Here we give the base case 50% and each edge case 25%. The probability-weighted value is:"},{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["0.25 × $35.86","+","0.50 × $26.03","+","0.25 × $13.56","≈","$25.37"],"caption":"The probability-weighted value per share."},{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"Bear","value":13.56},{"label":"Weighted","value":25.37},{"label":"Base","value":26.03},{"label":"Price today","value":32,"highlight":true},{"label":"Bull","value":35.86}],"unit":"$","caption":"Value per share in each scenario next to the $32 price: only the bull case is higher."},{"t":"p","text":"This chart is the heart of the thesis. At $32, even the bull case offers only 12% upside, while the bear case loses almost 58%. The odds are tilted against you. That is not a judgment about Clearbite the company, which looks excellent. It is a judgment about Clearbite the stock, at this price."},{"t":"check","q":"If the bear case happens and the stock falls from $32 to $13.56, what gain do you need just to get back to $32?","a":"About 136%. Losses and gains are not symmetric: 13.56 × 2.36 ≈ 32. This is the math of losses from the 301 lesson: the deeper the hole, the steeper the climb out."},{"t":"h","text":"How the picture changes with price"},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Price paid","Bull","Base","Bear"],"rows":[["$32","+12%","−19%","−58%"],["$26","+38%","0%","−48%"],["$22","+63%","+18%","−38%"],["$20","+79%","+30%","−32%"]],"caption":"Same company, same scenarios: only the price changes, and the odds flip."},{"t":"p","text":"At $20, the bull case gains 79% and the bear case loses 32%, an upside-to-downside ratio of about 2.5 to 1, and the weighted value sits about 27% above the price. The business did not change at all. Only the price did. This is the deepest lesson in stock investing: **the same company can be a poor investment or an attractive one depending only on what you pay**."},{"t":"exercise","title":"Re-weight the scenarios","prompt":"A skeptical colleague thinks DentaCore is stronger than you assume. She puts 20% on bull, 50% on base and 30% on bear. Compute the weighted value per share and the expected gain or loss from $32. Then find the price at which her weighted value would offer a 20% margin of safety.","steps":["Weighted value = 0.20 × 35.86 + 0.50 × 26.03 + 0.30 × 13.56.","Expected return = weighted value ÷ 32 − 1.","Price with 20% margin of safety = weighted value × 0.80."],"answer":"Weighted value: 7.17 + 13.02 + 4.07 ≈ $24.26\nFrom $32: 24.26 ÷ 32 − 1 ≈ −24%\nPrice for a 20% margin of safety: 24.26 × 0.80 ≈ $19.40\n\nReading: moving just 5 points of probability from bull to bear lowered the weighted value by about $1.10. Probabilities are soft, so test a few sets. If the conclusion (\"too expensive at $32\") survives every reasonable set, it is robust."},{"t":"warn","text":"Do not make the base case the average of bull and bear by default, and do not give it 80% to feel safe. If you cannot explain why a scenario deserves its probability in one sentence, the number is decoration."},{"t":"chapter","title":"Risks and the pre-mortem","minutes":7},{"t":"p","text":"Every thesis has a section on risks. Most are useless: a generic list of \"competition, regulation, the economy\" that could be pasted into any report. A useful risk section does three things. It names specific risks, estimates how much each would hurt, and says what early signal would tell you it is happening."},{"t":"h","text":"The pre-mortem"},{"t":"p","text":"Psychologist Gary Klein popularized a simple exercise. Imagine it is three years from now and this investment has been a painful failure. Now write the story of what went wrong. Because you assume the failure already happened, your brain stops defending the idea and starts explaining its collapse. People find far more risks this way than by asking \"what could go wrong?\""},{"t":"fig","kind":"chat","messages":[{"role":"you","text":"It is three years later and Clearbite is down 60%. What happened?"},{"role":"ai","text":"DentaCore gave its cloud version free for a year to its existing users. Switchers stayed with the brand they knew."},{"role":"you","text":"What else could explain it?"},{"role":"ai","text":"A big dental chain bought 1,500 practices and moved them all to its own system. Churn jumped to 11%."},{"role":"you","text":"And the numbers that would show it first?"},{"role":"ai","text":"Fewer new practices per quarter, rising sales cost per win, and churn above 9% for two quarters."}],"caption":"Running a pre-mortem with an AI assistant: useful for ideas, but you judge which are real."},{"t":"h","text":"The risk register"},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Risk","Likelihood","Impact","Early signal"],"rows":[["DentaCore bundles cloud","Medium","High","Win rate falls"],["Dental chains consolidate","Medium","Medium","Churn above 9%"],["Payments fee pressure","Low","Medium","Take rate drops"],["Data breach","Low","High","Security incident"],["Share dilution","Medium","Low","Shares up 2%+ a year"],["Key leader leaves","Low","Medium","CEO or CTO exit"]],"caption":"A risk register: specific, sized, and tied to a signal you can actually watch."},{"t":"fig","kind":"matrix","xAxis":"Likelihood →","yAxis":"Impact →","cells":[{"title":"Monitor","note":"Data breach, payments"},{"title":"Core risk","note":"DentaCore bundling"},{"title":"Accept","note":"Leader exit"},{"title":"Track","note":"Chains, dilution"}],"caption":"Sorting risks by likelihood and impact shows where to spend your attention."},{"t":"p","text":"The top-right box is where the thesis lives or dies. For Clearbite, that is DentaCore’s cloud move. If the practices still on DentaCore’s old software move to its new cloud product instead of to Clearbite, the bear case becomes the base case. Everything else is secondary."},{"t":"term","word":"Thesis-breaking risk","def":"A risk that, if it happens, does not just lower your estimate a little but invalidates the main reason you own the stock."},{"t":"h","text":"Risks you cannot see in the numbers"},{"t":"list","items":["**Management quality**: read three years of shareholder letters. Do leaders admit mistakes and explain them, or only celebrate?","**Incentives**: is executive pay tied to revenue growth at any cost, or to profit per share and returns on capital?","**Accounting choices**: how much software development is capitalized instead of expensed? Rising capitalization can flatter margins.","**Customer concentration**: does any single dental group bring more than 5% of revenue?"],"ordered":false},{"t":"exercise","title":"Run your own pre-mortem","prompt":"Write a five-sentence story of how an investment in Clearbite fails over three years, using a cause NOT already listed in the risk register. Then turn it into one row of the register: risk, likelihood, impact, and an early signal you could check every quarter.","answer":"Sample story: A new federal rule requires all dental records to be stored in a format that any system can open. Moving data between systems becomes easy and cheap. Rivals start offering free migration in a weekend. Clearbite’s churn rises from 7% to 13% within two years. Its switching-cost moat, the core of the thesis, shrinks sharply.\n\nRegister row:\nRisk: data portability regulation weakens switching costs\nLikelihood: Low to medium\nImpact: High (attacks the main moat)\nEarly signal: proposed rules in the news; rivals advertising free migration; churn trending above 9%\n\nThis is a good pre-mortem result because it targets the moat itself, not just a number in the model."},{"t":"chapter","title":"Position size: how much, if anything?","minutes":7},{"t":"p","text":"Suppose that after all this work, you decide a stock is attractive. The next question is not \"should I buy?\" but \"how much could I lose, and can I live with it?\" Position sizing is where analysis meets real risk. Two investors with the same thesis can have very different results simply because one bet 2% of their portfolio and the other bet 30%."},{"t":"h","text":"Size from the bear case, not from conviction"},{"t":"p","text":"Conviction feels like information, but it often is not. A disciplined approach starts from the downside: decide the maximum share of your portfolio you are willing to lose if the bear case happens, then work backward to the position size."},{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["Max position","=","Max loss you accept","÷","Bear-case drop"],"caption":"Position sizing that starts from what you can afford to lose."},{"t":"example","title":"Sizing Clearbite in a $100,000 portfolio","text":"Rule: no single bear case may cost more than 1.5% of the portfolio, that is $1,500.\nAt $32, the bear drop is to $13.56, a 57.6% loss.\nMax position: $1,500 ÷ 0.576 ≈ $2,600, or 2.6% of the portfolio.\n\nAt $20, the bear drop is 32.2%.\nMax position: $1,500 ÷ 0.322 ≈ $4,660, or 4.7%.\n\nSame rule, same company: a lower price allows a position almost twice as large, because the downside is smaller."},{"t":"p","text":"Add a hard ceiling on top. Many individual investors cap any single stock at around 5% of the portfolio, whatever the math says, because the bear case itself could be too optimistic. The numbers here are illustrative; the right limits depend on your situation, and most of a typical portfolio may sensibly sit in broad index funds."},{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"Buy at $32","value":2.6},{"label":"Buy at $24","value":3.4},{"label":"Buy at $20","value":4.7,"highlight":true},{"label":"Hard cap","value":5}],"unit":"%","caption":"Maximum position as a share of the portfolio under a 1.5% bear-case loss rule."},{"t":"h","text":"Correlation: hidden concentration"},{"t":"p","text":"If you already own VetLedger and MedSuite, adding Clearbite does not add a new, independent bet. All three sell software to small health practices and would fall together if, say, interest rates jumped and investors fled growth stocks. Count related positions as one group and cap the group too, for example at 12% to 15% of the portfolio."},{"t":"h","text":"Exits: thesis stops, not just price stops"},{"t":"p","text":"In the 301 lesson you met stop-losses: selling automatically if the price falls a set amount. They have a role, especially for traders. For a long-term thesis, a better tool is the **thesis stop**: a written list of business facts that, if they happen, mean you sell regardless of the price. A stock that falls 20% while every business signal improves may be a better deal than before. A stock that rises while the moat erodes may be a trap."},{"t":"fig","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Price stop","icon":"📉","items":["Sell if down 25%","Simple and automatic","Ignores the business","Can sell at the bottom"]},"right":{"title":"Thesis stop","icon":"📋","items":["Sell if churn > 10%","Needs monitoring","Tied to the reason you own it","Works in both directions"]},"caption":"Two ways to decide when to exit: by price or by business facts."},{"t":"exercise","title":"Size a position","prompt":"Lucia has a $60,000 portfolio. Her rule is that any single stock’s bear case may cost her at most 2% of the portfolio, with a hard cap of 6% per stock. She is considering Clearbite at $24. Using the bear value of $13.56, how much can she invest? She already holds $5,000 in VetLedger and caps health software as a group at 12%. Does that change the answer?","steps":["Max loss = 2% × $60,000.","Bear drop at $24 = (24 − 13.56) ÷ 24.","Max position = max loss ÷ bear drop; compare with the 6% hard cap.","Check the group cap: 12% × $60,000 minus VetLedger."],"answer":"Max loss: 2% × $60,000 = $1,200.\nBear drop at $24: 10.44 ÷ 24 ≈ 43.5%.\nMax position from the loss rule: 1,200 ÷ 0.435 ≈ $2,760 (4.6%).\nHard cap: 6% × 60,000 = $3,600, so the loss rule binds: $2,760.\nGroup cap: 12% × 60,000 = $7,200; minus $5,000 VetLedger = $2,200 left.\n\nAnswer: about $2,200, because the group cap is the tightest limit. Lesson: the smallest of your limits always wins."},{"t":"check","q":"Why size from the bear case instead of from how confident you feel?","a":"Because confidence is not the same as being right, and it tends to be highest just after you finish your own research. The bear case gives a number for how much you can lose, which is what actually decides whether a mistake is survivable."},{"t":"chapter","title":"The watch-list, the written thesis and the pre-buy checklist","minutes":8},{"t":"p","text":"You have done the work. Now you write it down in a form your future self can use. The final output of a thesis is not a price target. It is a short document that says what you believe, what you paid or would pay, and exactly what would change your mind, in both directions."},{"t":"h","text":"The watch-list: signals and thresholds"},{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Metric","Base expects","Worry if","Excited if"],"rows":[["Yearly churn","7%","Above 9%, 2 qtrs","Below 6%"],["Net new practices","1,000+ a year","Under 700","Over 1,600"],["Operating margin","+2 pts a year","Flat 2 years","+3 pts a year"],["Rev / practice","+5% a year","Under +3%","Over +6%"],["Share count","About flat","+2% a year or more","Falling"]],"caption":"The watch-list: every number from the model, with thresholds set in advance."},{"t":"p","text":"Setting thresholds _before_ results come out is the key. Once you own a stock, your brain will find a reason to excuse every bad number. Writing \"I worry if churn is above 9% for two quarters\" today protects you from yourself tomorrow."},{"t":"fig","kind":"timeline","events":[{"when":"Each quarter","label":"Results: churn, practices, margin"},{"when":"Month 3","label":"DentaCore cloud launch"},{"when":"Month 6","label":"Insurer integration renewal"},{"when":"Month 9","label":"Annual price increase"},{"when":"Month 12","label":"Full thesis review"}],"caption":"A year of scheduled moments when the thesis gets tested."},{"t":"h","text":"What would change my mind"},{"t":"list","items":["**More positive**: DentaCore’s cloud launch is late or poorly received; Clearbite reports churn under 6%; payments push revenue per practice up 7% or more; or the price falls to where the weighted value offers a margin of safety.","**More negative**: churn above 9% for two quarters; net new practices below 700 a year; margin flat for two years; or management starts using \"adjusted\" numbers that exclude growing costs.","**Thesis-breaking**: a rule that makes patient data trivially portable, or a large chain winning share with its own software."],"ordered":false},{"t":"h","text":"The written thesis in one screen"},{"t":"example","title":"Clearbite Systems: thesis summary (fictional, illustrative)","text":"Business: cloud software for US dental practices; subscription plus payments.\nIndustry: $2.5B market, 40% still on old software being retired.\nMoat: switching costs; churn 7%, price rises without churn.\nUnit economics: LTV ÷ CAC ≈ 8.7, payback 1.6 years.\nValue: DCF base $26.03; peers $27 to $29; weighted scenarios $25.37.\nPrice: $32. Reverse DCF needs 13.6% steady practice growth or a 29% margin.\nAsymmetry at $32: bull +12%, bear −58%.\nVerdict: excellent business, priced for the bull case. Watch-list, no position at this price.\nRevisit if: price nears a 20% margin of safety on weighted value, or margin evidence points to 28%+.\nKey risk: DentaCore bundling its own cloud product."},{"t":"p","text":"Notice the verdict. After hours of work, the conclusion is to wait. That is a perfectly good outcome. Most great investors say no far more often than yes. The work is not wasted: you now know Clearbite deeply, and if the price drops or the facts improve, you can act quickly and calmly while others panic or hesitate."},{"t":"fig","kind":"matrix","xAxis":"Price attractiveness →","yAxis":"Business quality →","cells":[{"title":"Watch-list","note":"Great business, rich price"},{"title":"Candidate","note":"Great business, fair price"},{"title":"Avoid","note":"Weak business, rich price"},{"title":"Value trap?","note":"Weak business, low price"}],"caption":"Where Clearbite sits today: top-left, a great business at a rich price."},{"t":"h","text":"The pre-buy checklist"},{"t":"p","text":"Pilots and surgeons use checklists not because they are beginners but because checklists catch the mistakes experts make when they are confident or rushed. Before you ever put money into a stock, run through these six groups of questions. If you cannot answer one, you are not finished."},{"t":"list","items":["**Business**: can I explain how it makes money in one sentence? Do I know its revenue drivers and which one is doing the work?","**Industry and moat**: is the market growing, and is the moat proven by a number (churn, pricing, margins over time)?","**Unit economics and balance sheet**: does each new customer pay back its cost, and could the company survive two bad years without raising money?","**Valuation**: have I run a DCF with a sensitivity table, cross-checked with peer multiples, and done a reverse DCF?","**Scenarios and risks**: do I have bull, base and bear with probabilities, and a risk register from a pre-mortem?","**Position and exits**: is my size set from the bear case and group limits, and is my watch-list with thresholds written down and dated?"],"ordered":true},{"t":"fig","kind":"pyramid","levels":["Position size + exits","Scenarios + risks","Valuation: DCF + multiples","Moat + unit economics","Business + industry"],"caption":"The checklist as a pyramid: never build the top on an unfinished base."},{"t":"exercise","title":"Run the checklist on Clearbite","prompt":"Go through the six checklist groups for Clearbite at $32 and mark each one as passed, failed or unclear, with one line of evidence. What does the result tell you?","answer":"1. Business: passed. Subscription plus payments; growth driven mainly by new practices.\n2. Industry and moat: passed. $2.5B market with forced switching; churn 7%, price rises held.\n3. Unit economics and balance sheet: passed. LTV ÷ CAC ≈ 8.7; $150M net cash, no debt.\n4. Valuation: failed at $32. DCF $26.03, peers $27 to $29, reverse DCF needs bullish inputs.\n5. Scenarios and risks: done, but unfavorable. Bull +12% vs bear −58%; core risk is DentaCore.\n6. Position and exits: done. Sizing rule allows only about 2.6% at $32; thesis stops written.\n\nResult: the business passes every quality test; the price fails. The checklist does not say \"never\"; it says \"not at this price, keep watching\". That is the checklist working as intended."},{"t":"h","text":"Closing the masterclass"},{"t":"p","text":"You have now done what professional analysts do: sized a market, tested a moat, built a model from drivers, valued it three ways, mapped the range of outcomes, named the risks, sized a position and written down what would change your mind. Most investors never do this for a single stock. The process will feel slow the first time. By the fifth company it takes an evening, and by the tenth it becomes a way of seeing."},{"t":"quote","text":"Price is what you pay. Value is what you get.","by":"Warren Buffett"},{"t":"tip","text":"Choose one public company you know as a customer. This week, complete just the first two checklist groups for it. Next week, add the model. Build the full thesis over a month, one subway ride at a time."}],"takeaways":["Start with the industry and prove the moat with numbers like churn and pricing power.","Build the model from drivers, then value it with a DCF, peer multiples and a reverse DCF.","Bull, base and bear scenarios reveal the asymmetry you buy at a given price.","Size positions from the bear case and group limits, never from how confident you feel.","Write a dated thesis with watch-list thresholds and run the pre-buy checklist every time."],"quiz":[{"q":"There are 125,000 practices and the average practice spends $20,000 a year on this kind of software. What is the total addressable market?","options":["$250M","$25B","$2.5B","$1.25B"],"answer":2,"why":"TAM = 125,000 × $20,000 = $2.5 billion a year. Clearbite’s $250M is 10% of it."},{"q":"Gross profit per practice is $15,200 a year and churn is 7%. Using the simple formula, what is lifetime value?","options":["About $217,000","About $106,000","About $15,200","About $1,064"],"answer":0,"why":"LTV ≈ yearly gross profit ÷ churn = 15,200 ÷ 0.07 ≈ $217,000. It ignores discounting, so treat it as an upper bound."},{"q":"Which piece of evidence best supports a switching-cost moat?","options":["The CEO says customers love the product","Prices rose and churn did not move","Revenue grew 17% last year","The stock price doubled"],"answer":1,"why":"If customers could leave easily, a price increase would raise churn. Pricing power with stable churn is hard evidence of switching costs."},{"q":"Year-5 free cash flow is $65.6M, the discount rate 9% and terminal growth 3%. What is the terminal value at the end of year 5?","options":["About $729M","About $2,252M","About $1,126M","About $656M"],"answer":2,"why":"TV = 65.6 × 1.03 ÷ (0.09 − 0.03) ≈ 67.6 ÷ 0.06 ≈ $1,126M."},{"q":"About 82% of Clearbite’s DCF value comes from the terminal value. What does that imply?","options":["The first five years do not matter at all","The result is very sensitive to the discount rate and long-term growth","The DCF must be wrong","The company should pay a dividend"],"answer":1,"why":"When most value sits in the terminal value, small changes in its inputs move the answer a lot, which is why a sensitivity table is essential."},{"q":"What does a reverse DCF do?","options":["It values the company backward from year 5 to year 1","It replaces the discount rate with the P/E","It works out what assumptions the current price implies","It removes the terminal value"],"answer":2,"why":"It solves for the growth, margin or discount rate that makes today’s price fair, turning the price into business claims you can judge."},{"q":"Bull is worth $35.86, base $26.03 and bear $13.56, weighted 25%, 50%, 25%. What is the weighted value?","options":["About $25.37","About $26.03","About $28.00","About $24.26"],"answer":0,"why":"0.25 × 35.86 + 0.50 × 26.03 + 0.25 × 13.56 ≈ 8.97 + 13.02 + 3.39 ≈ $25.37."},{"q":"Your rule is a maximum bear-case loss of $1,500. The bear case implies a 57.6% drop. What is your maximum position?","options":["About $864","About $2,600","About $3,500","About $1,500"],"answer":1,"why":"Max position = max loss ÷ bear drop = 1,500 ÷ 0.576 ≈ $2,600."},{"q":"You own two health-software stocks and are adding a third in the same niche. What is the main risk?","options":["Too much diversification","Higher trading fees","Lower dividends","Hidden concentration: they may fall together"],"answer":3,"why":"Similar businesses react to the same forces, so they behave like one bigger bet. Cap the group, not only each stock."},{"q":"After a full analysis, a great business looks priced for its bull case. What is the most disciplined conclusion?","options":["Put it on a watch-list with written thresholds and revisit at a lower price or better facts","Buy a large position because quality always wins","Short the stock immediately","Delete the analysis and move on"],"answer":0,"why":"Quality and price are separate questions. Saying \"not at this price\" while tracking clear triggers keeps the work useful and the risk controlled."}]}]});
